%!testlib
% Collecting over a dictionary whose hash table is larger than a
% composite's size field can count, run through `xpost -d null` by
% run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every assertion holds.
%
% A dictionary's storage is a table of 2*sz+1 records, so the largest
% table index is more than twice the size the sz field carries. In the
% default build that field is 16 bits wide, so a dictionary the field
% describes perfectly well has a table the field cannot index -- and
% anything that walks the table with a counter of that width never
% reaches the end of it. The collector walks every reachable dictionary.
%
% The sizes below are chosen to straddle that boundary in the default
% build and to be unremarkable in the large-object build, so the same
% assertions hold in both. A dictionary reaches the far side of it two
% ways -- asked for at that size, or grown into it -- and both are here.
% The collections asked for by operator are here: local VM, local and
% global, and one with a save outstanding. A collection the interpreter
% starts for itself takes the same walk, and gc_large_dict_pressure_test
% provokes one -- the ordinary threshold is far too high to reach from a
% test, so a section here that merely allocated would assert nothing.

% The table of a dict asked for at this size spans 65537 records: one
% more than the default build's size field can count.
/bigsz 26214 def

% --- an explicit collection of local VM walks the whole table ---
/D bigsz dict def
D /probe 42 put
1 vmreclaim
D /probe get 42 eq (a local collection walks an oversized dict table) assert
D length 1 eq (the dict survives the collection intact) assert

% --- and so does a collection of local and global VM ---
2 vmreclaim
D /probe get 42 eq (a global collection walks an oversized dict table) assert

% --- a dict that reaches that size by growing is the same dict ---
% growth over-allocates, so the table crosses the boundary well before
% the entry count does
/GD 4 dict def
0 1 15500 { GD exch dup put } for
1 vmreclaim
GD length 15501 eq (a grown dict keeps every entry across a collection) assert
GD 0 get 0 eq (the first key survives the collection) assert
GD 15500 get 15500 eq (the last key survives the collection) assert

% --- and one with a save outstanding walks it too ---
save
/SD bigsz dict def
SD /probe 9 put
1 vmreclaim
SD /probe get 9 eq (a collection under save walks the table) assert
restore
D /probe get 42 eq (the pre-save dict is intact after restore) assert

% --- a dict asked for at the size field's limit is that size, and
%     counts every entry it takes ---
% The table is over-allocated by a quarter, so the over-allocated size
% of the largest dict the size field can describe does not itself fit in
% that field; the count of entries in use is held in a field of the same
% width again. A dict that keeps taking entries past what the count
% field holds reads back as a nearly empty dict holding tens of
% thousands of retrievable keys; a build whose fields cannot reach that
% far must refuse the entry instead. A dict that truncated the
% over-allocation runs out of table where its own capacity says there is
% room, and doubles the table to find some.
/CD 65535 dict def
CD maxlength 65535 eq (a dict at the size field's limit reports its capacity) assert
/naccept 0 def
mark
{ 0 1 65600 { CD exch dup put /naccept naccept 1 add store } for } stopped
{ $error /errorname get /limitcheck eq
    (a dict that can take no more entries raises limitcheck) assert } if
cleartomark
CD maxlength naccept ge (a dict at the size field's limit has room for all it took) assert
CD maxlength 65535 2 mul lt (a dict at the size field's limit does not grow early) assert
CD length naccept eq (a dict reports every entry it took) assert
1 vmreclaim
CD 0 get 0 eq (the first entry survives a collection) assert
CD 20000 get 20000 eq (and so does one from the middle) assert

verdict
flush
quit
